  "SubCat01_Header": "WHAT IS AUTOGENIC TRAINING",
  "SubCat01_Text": "Autogenic Training is a scientifically recognized relaxation method developed in the 1920s by the Berlin psychiatrist Johannes Heinrich Schultz. It is based on self-suggestion: by repeatedly mentally reciting certain formulas (\"My arm is very heavy\", \"My breathing is calm and even\"), one puts body and mind into a state of deep relaxation. <br><br><strong>Effects on Body and Mind</strong> <br>Autogenic Training can reduce stress, relieve inner tension, and enhance concentration. When practiced regularly, it supports regeneration, improves sleep, and can alleviate psychosomatic complaints. On a mental level, it helps to organize thoughts, find inner calm, and enhance well-being.\n\n<b>Influence on the Autonomic Nervous System</b>\n The autonomic nervous system controls vital bodily functions such as heart rate, breathing, blood pressure, and digestion – largely without our conscious intervention. Autogenic Training has a regulating effect on this system by activating the parasympathetic nervous system, which is responsible for relaxation and recovery. At the same time, the excessive activity of the sympathetic nervous system – responsible for stress reactions such as rapid heartbeat or muscle tension – is reduced. \n\n <strong>Examples of Control through Autogenic Training</strong><ul><li><strong>Cardiovascular System:</strong> By using the formula 'My heart beats calmly and regularly', pulse rate can decrease, and blood pressure and heart rate stabilize.</li><li><strong>Breathing:</strong> With 'My breathing flows calmly and evenly', breathing can be deepened and slowed, promoting inner calm.</li><li><strong>Muscles:</strong> 'My arms and legs are pleasantly heavy' can relieve muscle tension and induce physical relaxation.</li><li><strong>Temperature Regulation:</strong> 'My right arm is very warm' influences blood circulation and can warm cold hands or feet. Through this self-regulation, Autogenic Training becomes an effective tool for bringing body and mind into harmony and building long-term stress resistance.</li></ul>",
  
